;;; A wildcard `_` in a rule's `:match` matches nothing —
;;; regression `wild-in-match` (`corpus/regressions.toml`); a bare
;;; `_` is reserved and refused (Q-M1e2.22).
;;;
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds a)`, and one rule that concludes `(hit w)` from any
;;; `holds` fact: its pattern's one argument is the wildcard `_`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A bare `_` is reserved and refused wherever it is written (Q-M1e2.22):
;;; the program is refused at the parse, at the `_`, on every command. With
;;; `_` written as a variable the rule reads nowhere else
;;; (`wild-in-match-twin.ein`), `w` fires on `(holds a)` and root holds
;;; `(hit w)`; the blind enumerator proposes `(hit a)`, so R is two states,
;;; `{(hit w)}` and `{(hit a), (hit w)}`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and what it answers ──────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `c73a0796`, release and debug (every mode and `ein oracle` the same),
;;; `w` never fired: the rule compiler lowered `_` as an opaque slot, which
;;; unifies with nothing (`compile.rs`' `slot`). Root held no `hit`, and R
;;; was `∅` and `{(hit a)}`, no state holding `(hit w)` (`ein solve FILE`).
;;; Today the parse refuses the `_`.
;;;

(relation holds Node) (relation hit Node)
(holds a)
(rule w () :match (holds _) :assert (hit w))
